<!--
    GH-841 (kuna): "PowerPC -O0: a variadic call drops a trailing double whose
    register also feeds an earlier argument".
    https://github.com/Noelo-Lab/kuna/issues/841

    Source (clang -O0 -fno-pic -target powerpc-linux-gnu -c, the .text bytes
    verbatim at 0x100000; the same at -O2 at 0x101000, functions suffixed _o2):

        static __attribute__((noinline)) int sumi(int n, ...) { ... va_arg(ap, double) ... }
        int a9(double x) { return sumi(2, x * 2, x); }
        int a7(double x, double y) { return sumi(3, x * 2, y, x); }
        int a5(double x) { return sumi(2, -x, x); }
        int b1(double x) { return sumi(2, x, x * 2); }
        int b2(double x) { double t = x * 2; return sumi(2, t, x); }
        int p1(double *a, double *b, double *c) { return sumi(1, *a * *b + *c); }
        int p4(double *a, double *b, double *c) { return sumi(1, *c - *a * *b); }

    a9 is `lfd 2,16(31); fadd 1,2,2; li 3,2; crset 6; bl sumi`: x is loaded
    once into f2 and x + x is computed from it into f1.  Upstream's
    checkCallDoubleUse refuses a value that also feeds another active argument,
    so the f2 trial is dropped.  p1 (`lfd 0; lfd 1; lfd 2; fmadd 1,0,1,2`) and
    p4 are the same shape with f2 a scratch: f0 is busy there, so the register
    choice does not say f2 was passed.  b1 and b2 are already right.

    Stage model: P4 call recovery, active-input-trial scoring.
    P0 assertion: option varargsharedfloat on|off (default off).
    Pass 1 (default, off) reproduces the bug: a9 prints sumi(2,x + x), a7
    drops its trailing x, a5 its x.  Pass 2 (on) passes them; b1, b2, p1, p4
    and the -O2 copies print the same as with the option off.
-->
